#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:1
msgid "How to write documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:3
msgid ""
"Good documentation is not natural.  There are opposing goals that make "
"writing good documentation difficult.  It requires expertise in the subject "
"but also writing to a novice perspective.  Documentation therefore often "
"glazes over implementation detail, or leaves readers with unanswered "
"questions."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:8
msgid ""
"There are a few tenets to Rust documentation that can help guide anyone "
"through the process of documenting libraries so that everyone has an ample "
"opportunity to use the code."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:12
msgid ""
"This chapter covers not only how to write documentation but specifically how "
"to write **good** documentation.  It is important to be as clear as you can, "
"and as complete as possible.  As a rule of thumb: the more documentation you "
"write for your crate the better.  If an item is public then it should be "
"documented."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:18
msgid "Getting Started"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:20
msgid ""
"Documenting a crate should begin with front-page documentation.  As an "
"example, the [`hashbrown`](https://docs.rs/hashbrown/0.8.2/hashbrown/) crate "
"level documentation summarizes the role of the crate, provides links to "
"explain technical details, and explains why you would want to use the crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:25
msgid ""
"After introducing the crate, it is important that the front-page gives an "
"example of how to use the crate in a real world setting.  Stick to the "
"library's role in the example, but do so without shortcuts to benefit users "
"who may copy and paste the example to get started."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:30
msgid ""
"[`futures`](https://docs.rs/futures/0.3.5/futures/) uses inline comments to "
"explain line by line the complexities of using a [`Future`](https://doc.rust-"
"lang.org/std/future/trait.Future.html), because a person's first exposure to "
"rust's [`Future`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/future/trait.Future.html) "
"may be this example."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:34
msgid ""
"The [`backtrace`](https://docs.rs/backtrace/0.3.50/backtrace/) documentation "
"walks through the whole process, explaining changes made to the `Cargo.toml` "
"file, passing command line arguments to the compiler, and shows a quick "
"example of backtrace in the wild."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:38
msgid ""
"Finally, the front-page can eventually become a comprehensive reference how "
"to use a crate, like [`regex`](https://docs.rs/regex/1.3.9/regex/).  In this "
"front page, all requirements are outlined, the edge cases shown, and "
"practical examples provided.  The front page goes on to show how to use "
"regular expressions then concludes with crate features."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:44
msgid ""
"Don't worry about comparing your crate, which is just beginning, to other "
"more developed crates.  To get the documentation to something more polished, "
"start incrementally and put in an introduction, example, and features.  Rome "
"was not built in a day!"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:49
msgid ""
"The first lines within the `lib.rs` will compose the front-page, and they "
"use a different convention than the rest of the rustdocs.  Lines should "
"start with `//!` which indicate module-level or crate-level documentation. "
"Here's a quick example of the difference:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:55
msgid ""
"//! Fast and easy queue abstraction.\n"
"//!\n"
"//! Provides an abstraction over a queue.  When the abstraction is used\n"
"//! there are these advantages:\n"
"//! - Fast\n"
"//! - [`Easy`]\n"
"//!\n"
"//! [`Easy`]: http://thatwaseasy.example.com\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:63
msgid "/// This module makes it easy.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:67
msgid "/// Use the abstraction function to do this specific thing.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:73
msgid ""
"Ideally, this first line of documentation is a sentence without highly "
"technical details, but with a good description of where this crate fits "
"within the rust ecosystem.  Users should know whether this crate meets their "
"use case after reading this line."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:78
msgid "Documenting components"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:80
msgid ""
"Whether it is modules, structs, functions, or macros: the public API of all "
"code should have documentation. Rarely does anyone complain about too much "
"documentation!"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:84
msgid ""
"It is recommended that each item's documentation follows this basic "
"structure:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:96
msgid ""
"This basic structure should be straightforward to follow when writing your "
"documentation; while you might think that a code example is trivial, the "
"examples are really important because they can help users understand what an "
"item is, how it is used, and for what purpose it exists."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:101
msgid ""
"Let's see an example coming from the [standard library](https://doc.rust-"
"lang.org/stable/std/index.html) by taking a look at the [`std::env::args()`]"
"(https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/env/fn.args.html) function:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:136
msgid ""
"Everything before the first empty line will be reused to describe the "
"component in searches and module overviews.  For example, the function `std::"
"env::args()` above will be shown on the [`std::env`](https://doc.rust-lang."
"org/stable/std/env/index.html#functions) module documentation. It is good "
"practice to keep the summary to one line: concise writing is a goal of good "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:142
msgid ""
"Because the type system does a good job of defining what types a function "
"passes and returns, there is no benefit of explicitly writing it into the "
"documentation, especially since `rustdoc` adds hyper links to all types in "
"the function signature."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:146
msgid ""
"In the example above, a 'Panics' section explains when the code might "
"abruptly exit, which can help the reader prevent reaching a panic.  A panic "
"section is recommended every time edge cases in your code can be reached if "
"known."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-write-documentation.md:150
msgid ""
"As you can see, it follows the structure detailed above: it starts with a "
"short sentence explaining what the functions does, then it provides more "
"information and finally provides a code example."
msgstr ""

